Weighted arc-reversal metagraph (row = class of a representative, entry = # of its 6 arcs
whose reversal lands in the column class):
                           TT4   A=C3+source     B=C3+sink   S4 (strong)
TT4                          3             1             1             1
A=C3+source                  3             0             0             3
B=C3+sink                    3             0             0             3
S4 (strong)                  1             1             1             3
Labeled-flow symmetry labeled(r)*W[r][c] = labeled(c)*W[c][r] holds for all pairs.

Converse quotient (merge A and B into M):
  TT4  -> TT4:3, M:2, S4:1
  M    -> TT4:3, M:0, S4:3
  S4   -> TT4:1, M:2, S4:3
  Quotient is a TRIANGLE (every pair adjacent) with loops at TT4 and S4 and no loop at M.

Edge-rotation graph of the 3 trees on 5 vertices (remove one edge, add one edge, stay a tree):
  path P_5               -> path P_5:8, spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1]:8
  spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1] -> path P_5:8, spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1]:5, star K_{1,4}:1
  star K_{1,4}           -> spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1]:12
  Rotation graph is a PATH (path -- fork -- star) with loops at all three nodes: NOT the triangle above.

==============================================================================
D. Erdos--Sos at t = 5: ex(n, T) for the three trees, n <= 7 (exhaustive over the graph atlas)
==============================================================================
n   3n/2    floor  | ex(n,star)  ex(n,fork)  ex(n,path)  | 3-regular graph on n exists?  extremal graphs attaining floor(3n/2)
5     7.5     7   |         7          6           6   |  NO (handshake: 3n odd)  attained by: ['star K_{1,4}']
      witness for path P_5              : 6 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 0], components [4, 1]
      witness for spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1]: 6 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 0], components [4, 1]
      witness for star K_{1,4}          : 7 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 2], components [5]
6     9.0     9   |         9          7           7   |  yes                     attained by: ['star K_{1,4}']
      witness for path P_5              : 7 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 1, 1], components [4, 2]
      witness for spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1]: 7 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 1, 1], components [4, 2]
      witness for star K_{1,4}          : 9 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3], components [6]
7    10.5    10   |        10          9           9   |  NO (handshake: 3n odd)  attained by: ['star K_{1,4}']
      witness for path P_5              : 9 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2], components [4, 3]
      witness for spider/fork deg=[3, 2, 1, 1, 1]: 9 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2, 2], components [4, 3]
      witness for star K_{1,4}          : 10 edges, degrees [3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2], components [7]
Erdos--Sos for all trees on 5 vertices is a THEOREM (paths: Erdos--Gallai 1959; stars: degree count;
fork = diameter-3 spider: McLennan 2005 / spider results).  The bound 3n/2 is attained with equality
only when 3n is even; the star attains floor(3n/2) for every n >= 5 (near-regular graphs of max degree 3);
the path and fork attain 3n/2 only at 4 | n (disjoint K4's) in the range checked.

==============================================================================
E. The forcing threshold E*(n) = floor(3n/2)+1 as a Collatz composite (all n <= 10^6)
==============================================================================
odd n : E*(n) = (3n+1)/2  = halve after 3x+1  (the Collatz odd step T(n))
even n: E*(n) = 3(n/2)+1  = 3x+1 after halving (3x+1 applied to T(n) = n/2)
Both are the two orders of composing {x -> 3x+1, x -> x/2}; this is shared syntax ((t-2)/2 = 3/2
and rounding), not a transfer of structure.  Typed: ELEMENTARY IDENTITY / SYNTAX-LEVEL.

mod-4 modes of 3n/2 at t = 5:
  n= 4  3n/2=  6.0  E*=  7  0 mod 4: whole, (t-1)|n, disjoint K4 extremal for ALL three trees
  n= 5  3n/2=  7.5  E*=  8  odd    : half, no 3-regular graph (handshake), star alone reaches the floor
  n= 6  3n/2=  9.0  E*= 10  2 mod 4: whole, 3-regular graphs exist but (t-1) does not divide n: star-only equality
  n= 7  3n/2= 10.5  E*= 11  odd    : half, no 3-regular graph (handshake), star alone reaches the floor
  n= 8  3n/2= 12.0  E*= 13  0 mod 4: whole, (t-1)|n, disjoint K4 extremal for ALL three trees
  n= 9  3n/2= 13.5  E*= 14  odd    : half, no 3-regular graph (handshake), star alone reaches the floor
  n=10  3n/2= 15.0  E*= 16  2 mod 4: whole, 3-regular graphs exist but (t-1) does not divide n: star-only equality
  n=11  3n/2= 16.5  E*= 17  odd    : half, no 3-regular graph (handshake), star alone reaches the floor
  n=12  3n/2= 18.0  E*= 19  0 mod 4: whole, (t-1)|n, disjoint K4 extremal for ALL three trees

